    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1032">

                <pb facs="0892" n="776" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg776"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/3533.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Consul at Chihuahua to the Secretary of
                        State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Consulate</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Chihuahua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-04-07T22:00:00-07:04">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 7, 1912—10 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> (Received, via El Paso, Tex., April 8, 1912—8.45 p.m.)</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Reported Haberiline,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1032-f1"/> American, arrested for
                    attempting leave Jimenez contrary orders rebel commander last Friday. Still
                    incarcerated incommunicado. Contrary statement newspapers; no appeal received
                    from prisoner this office and, inferring refusal permission on the part of
                    rebels, I wrote Orozco to-day urgently requesting immediate statement, but
                    communication apparently ignored. Rebels all sullen because of accepted belief
                    partiality of United States for established Government.</p>
                <p>Rebel chiefs issued manifesto yesterday repudiating Vásquez Gómez entirely.</p>
                <p>Orozco has taken no action repudiating Gonzalo Enrile’s letter abusive of the
                    United States, addressed to the President, reprinted and circulated here. But he
                    repudiates the likewise abusive manifesto circulated at the same time.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Letcher.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1032-f1" n="1" xml:id="d1032-f1-content">
                <p>Spelled Heberlein in later telegrams.</p>
            </note>
